# 2. Systemd Timer — Safe, Minimal Automation

This gives you **weekly backups**, but only when the drive is present.

---

## Step 1: Create Backup Script

### File:


```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

BACKUP_MOUNT="/media/$USER/BACKUP_PRIMARY"
REPO="$BACKUP_MOUNT/restic"

if [ ! -d "$BACKUP_MOUNT" ]; then
  echo "Backup drive not mounted. Exiting."
  exit 1
fi

export RESTIC_REPOSITORY="$REPO"
export RESTIC_PASSWORD_FILE="/home/$USER/.config/restic/passphrase"

restic backup \
  /home/$USER/Documents \
  /home/$USER/Notes

sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/restic-backup.sh

## Passphrase handling (recommended)

mkdir -p ~/.config/restic
chmod 700 ~/.config/restic
nano ~/.config/restic/passphrase
chmod 600 ~/.config/restic/passphrase

## Step 2: systemd Service

~/.config/systemd/user/restic-backup.service


### File

[Unit]
Description=Restic Backup (Local)

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/restic-backup.sh

## Step 3: systemd Timer

### File

~/.config/systemd/user/restic-backup.timer

---

[Unit]
Description=Weekly Restic Backup

[Timer]
OnCalendar=weekly
Persistent=true

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target


## Step 4: Enable Timer

```bash
systemctl --user daemon-reload
systemctl --user enable --now restic-backup.timer
systemctl --user list-timers

### Expected: Timer listed | Next run scheduled

## Step 5: Manual Test Run

```bash

systemctl --user start restic-backup.service

### Verify

```bash
restic snapshots

## Automation Exit Criteria : Timer scheduled: Manual run succeeds : Snapshot appears : Failure when drive is absent is clean and obvious






